<exercise label="parsons-expectation-proof-discrete-distractors">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>Y=aX+b</m> be a linear transformation of random variable <m>X</m>, where <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are constants. Then, 
            <me>E(Y)=E(aX + b) = aE(X) + b</me>. 
            </p>    
            
            <p>Reorder the blocks below to prove that this property is true. (Go step by step! Don't skip or combine steps.) You may or may not use all provided blocks.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks>
        <block order="1">
            <p>
                By definition, 
                <me>E(aX + b) = \sum_{x} (ax + b)P(X = x)</me>
            </p>
        </block>
        <block order="2" >
            <p>
              <m>  = \sum_{x} \left[ axP(X = x) + bP(X = x) \right] \quad</m> (distribute)
            </p>
        </block>
        <block order="3">
            <p>
                <m> = \sum_{x} axP(X = x) + \sum_{x} bP(X = x) \quad</m> (property of sums)
            </p>
        </block>
        <block order="4">
            <p>

                <me> = a \sum_{x} xP(X = x) + b \sum_{x} P(X = x) \quad</me> (property of sums)
            </p>
        </block>
        <block order="5">
            <p>
                
                <me> = aE(X)  + b \sum_{x} P(X = x) \quad</me> (by definition)
            </p>
        </block>
                <block order="6">
            <p>
                <me>  = aE(X) + b(1) = aE(X) + b,</me>
                since <m>\sum_{x} P(X = x)=1</m>
            </p>
        </block>
        <!-- Distractor 1 -->
        <block  correct="no">
            <p>

                <m>= \sum_{x} axP(X = x) + bP(X = x) \quad</m> (distribute)
            </p>
        </block>
        <!-- Distractor 2 -->
        <block correct="no">
            <p>

                <m>= \left(\sum_{all \, x} (ax + b)\right) \cdot \left(\sum_{all \, x} P(X = x)\right)\quad </m> (property of sums)
            </p>
        </block>
    </blocks>
</exercise>


<exercise xml:id="ex-variance-shortcut-proof-parsons">
    <title>Interactive Proof: The Variance Shortcut Formula</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The variance of a random variable <m>X</m> measures the expected value of the squared deviations around its mean, <m>\mu = E(X)</m>. Thus, the formula for variance can be written as:
            <me>V(X) = E\left[(X - E(X))^2\right] = E\left[(X - \mu)^2\right]</me>
            
            Prove that, from this definition, we can derive the variance shortcut formula:
            <me>V(X) = E(X^2) - [E(X)]^2 =  E(X^2) - \mu^2  </me>
        </p>
        
        <p>
            Arrange the blocks below in the correct logical sequence. You may or may not use all blocks.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks>
        <block order="1">
            <p>
             Expand the perfect square polynomial inside the expectation brackets:
            <me>\implies V(X) = E\left[X^2 - 2\mu X + \mu^2\right]</me>
        </p>
        </block>
        <!-- Step 2 -->
        <block order="2">
            <p>
                Apply the linearity property of expectations to split the expression into three terms:
                <me>
                    \implies V(X)  = E(X^2) - E(2\mu X) + E(\mu^2)
                </me>
            </p>
        </block>
        
        <!-- Step 3 -->
        <block order="3">
            <p>
                Apply the linearity property of expectations to handle the constant terms and simplify the expression:
                <me>
                  \implies V(X)  = E(X^2) - 2\mu E(X) + \mu^2
                </me>
            </p>
        </block>
        
        <!-- Step 4 -->
        <block order="4">
            <p>
                Substitute the identity <m>E(X) = \mu</m> into the linear expression:
                <me>
                   \implies V(X) = E(X^2) - 2\mu(\mu) + \mu^2 
                </me>
            </p>
        </block>

        <!-- Step 5 -->
        <block order="4">
            <p>
                Combine like terms:
                <me>
                    \implies V(X) = E(X^2) - 2\mu^2 + \mu^2 = E(X^2) - \mu^2
                </me>
            </p>
        </block>

        <!-- DISTRACTOR (Incorrect linearity handling) -->
        <block  correct="no">
            <p>
                Distribute the expectation operator inside the square brackets:
                <me>
                   \implies V(X)  = \left[E(X) - E(\mu)\right]^2 
                </me>
            </p>
        </block>
    </blocks>
    
    <hint>
        <p>
            Remember that the expectation operator <m>E</m> is linear, so it distributes across addition and subtraction. Once distributed, remember that <m>\mu</m> and <m>\mu^2</m> are static numbers, which means <m>E(\mu^2) = \mu^2</m>. Look for the block that distributes the expectation operator across the polynomial first.
        </p>
    </hint>
</exercise>




   <exercise xml:id="ex-mgf-sum-independent-proof">
    <title>Interactive Proof: MGF of a Sum of Independent Variables</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>Y = X_1 + X_2 + \dots + X_n</m>, where the <m>X_i</m>'s are mutually independent random variables. Prove the multiplicative property for moment generating functions:
            <me>M_Y(s) = M_{X_1}(s) \cdot M_{X_2}(s) \dots M_{X_n}(s)</me>
        </p>
        
        <p>
            Arrange the blocks below in the correct logical sequence. You will use all blocks. Start with the definition of a moment generating function.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks>
<block order="1">
    <p>
            By definition of a moment generating function:
            <me>M_Y(s) = E\left[e^{sY}\right]</me>
        </p>
</block>

<block order="2">
    <p>
            Substitute <m>Y = X_1 + X_2 + \dots + X_n</m>:
            <me>\implies M_Y(s) = E\left[e^{s(X_1 + X_2 + \dots + X_n)}\right]</me>
        </p>
</block>

        <!-- Step 2 -->
        <block order="3">
            <p>
                Distribute the <m>s</m>:
                <me>
                    \implies M_Y(s)  = E\left[e^{sX_1 + sX_2 + \dots + sX_n}\right]
                </me>
            </p>
        </block>


                <block order="4">
            <p>
                Apply laws of exponents:
                <me>
                    \implies M_Y(s)  = E\left[e^{sX_1} \cdot e^{sX_2} \dots e^{sX_n}\right]
                </me>
            </p>
        </block>
        
        <!-- Step 3 -->
        <block order="5">
            <p>
                Apply the expectation property for independent random variables:
                <me>
                  \implies M_Y(s)  = E\left[e^{sX_1}\right] \cdot E\left[e^{sX_2}\right] \dots E\left[e^{sX_n}\right]
                </me>
            </p>
        </block>
        
        <!-- Step 4 -->
        <block order="6">
            <p>
                We can now rewrite the expression as:
                <me>
                   \implies M_Y(s) = M_{X_1}(s) \cdot M_{X_2}(s) \dots M_{X_n}(s),
                </me>
                since <m>M_{X_i}(s) = E\left[e^{sX_i}\right]</m> (by definition).
            </p>
        </block>

    </blocks>
    
    <hint>
        <p>
            After using exponent laws to write the expression as a product of exponentials, you must apply the special expectation property that is only valid when variables are explicitly given as <term>independent</term>.
        </p>
    </hint>
</exercise>

