<exercise xml:id="ex-mgf-linear-transform-proof-static">
    <title>Proof: Linear Transformations of MGFs</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>X</m> be a random variable with moment generating function <m>M_X(s)</m>, and let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be constant scalars. Prove the linear transformation property for moment generating functions:
            <me>M_{aX+b}(s) = e^{bs}M_X(as)</me>
        </p>
    </statement>
    
    <hint>
        <p>
            Start with the definition of a moment generating function, <m>M_Z(s) = E\left[e^{sZ}\right]</m>, substituting <m>Z = aX + b</m>. Distribute the parameter <m>s</m> inside the exponent, split the exponential term into a product, and identify which component behaves as a deterministic constant relative to the expectation operator.
        </p>
    </hint>

    <solution>
        <p>
            To prove the property, we evaluate the expression directly from the foundational definition of a moment generating function:
        </p>
        <p>
            1. <term>Apply the MGF Definition:</term> Express the moment generating function of the transformed variable as an expectation:
            <me>M_{aX+b}(s) = E\left[e^{s(aX + b)}\right]</me>
        </p>
        <p>
            2. <term>Distribute and Split the Exponents:</term> Apply the algebraic distributive law inside the exponent power. Then, use the laws of exponents to convert the single exponential of a sum into a product of two distinct exponential bases:
            <md>
                <mrow>M_{aX+b}(s) \amp = E\left[e^{asX + bs}\right]</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp = E\left[e^{asX} \cdot e^{bs}\right]</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            3. <term>Invoke the Linearity of Expectation:</term> The term <m>e^{bs}</m> contains only constant parameters and is completely free of the random variable <m>X</m>. Relative to the expectation operator, it behaves as a deterministic scalar and can be factored completely outside the bracket:
            <me>M_{aX+b}(s) = e^{bs} \cdot E\left[e^{asX}\right]</me>
        </p>
        <p>
            4. <term>Identify the Resulting MGF:</term> Recognize that the remaining expression, <m>E\left[e^{(as)X}\right]</m>, matches the exact functional definition of the moment generating function for the base variable <m>X</m>, evaluated at the scaled parameter input <m>as</m> instead of <m>s</m>:
            <me>E\left[e^{(as)X}\right] = M_X(as)</me>
            Substituting this back into our equation completes the proof:
            <me>M_{aX+b}(s) = e^{bs} \cdot M_X(as)</me>
        </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>



   </exercises>

